{ id:'enthd-nose-341', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-sinusitis',
  stem:'A 33-year-old male patient presented with cheek and ear pain, nasal obstruction and **unilateral offensive rhinorrhea for 3 months**. What’s your provisional diagnosis?',
  options:['Nasal polyps','Atrophic rhinitis','Chronic maxillary sinusitis of dental origin','Chronic pansinusitis'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**Three months makes it chronic, and UNILATERAL plus OFFENSIVE narrows a chronic maxillary sinusitis to a local cause in the floor of the sinus — the teeth.**\n\n**The discharge is the diagnostic word.** `L31) nasal symptoms 1.pdf` prints a four-item list for foetid (bad-smelling) nasal discharge — *"1. Foreign bodies in the nose 2. **Odontogenic sinusitis** 3. Sinonasal neoplasms 4. Fungal infections"* — and separately makes laterality decisive: *"Unilateral nasal obstruction usually points to structural abnormality or uni-lateral pathology (antrochoanal polyp, **odontogenic pathology**)"*. In an adult, foetid + unilateral is dental sinusitis until proved otherwise; in a child the same combination is a **foreign body** (`entep-nose-71`).\n\n**The timing fits.** `L27.2) Chronic Sinusitis.pdf` defines chronic rhinosinusitis as *"chronic inflammatory disease of sinus mucosa lasting more than **12 weeks**"* and lists among its triggers *"Infection of an adjacent organ (e.g. **teeth**)"*. Three months is exactly that threshold. The **ear pain** is referred, not otitis: maxillary sinus pain travels along the **maxillary division of the trigeminal nerve**, the same pathway keyed in `enthd-nose-339`.\n\n| Option | Why it fails this case |\n|---|---|\n| **Nasal polyps** | **bilateral** and painless; the discharge is mucoid or watery, never offensive. `L26) nasal masses.pdf` files diffuse polyposis under *bilateral* masses, and the classic presentation is painless obstruction with hyposmia (`entep-nose-86`) |\n| **Atrophic rhinitis** | it does smell — *"crusts that emit a characteristic foul odor (**ozaena**)"* — but it is **bilateral**, the nose is abnormally **roomy** rather than obstructed, and `L29) Rhinitis 1.pdf` gives it *"abnormal patency of nasal passages"*. Nasal obstruction here is the paradoxical, subjective kind, not a blocked cheek |\n| **Chronic maxillary sinusitis of dental origin** | ✅ unilateral, offensive, cheek pain, 3 months — the whole quartet |\n| **Chronic pansinusitis** | *pan-* means every sinus, so the picture would be **bilateral**, with frontal and ethmoid pain too. One cheek does not make a pansinusitis |\n\n**Bottom line: unilateral offensive discharge in an adult sends you to the upper teeth.** The **upper first molar** is the tooth most intimately related to the maxillary sinus (`entep-nose-62`), and the discharge is characteristically **unilateral and foetid** (`entep-nose-54`). The endpoint bank prints this same vignette at 12 weeks and keys it identically (`entep-nose-66`), and `entep-nose-37` keys dental sinusitis for unilateral offensive discharge in an adult. Treatment fails until the tooth is dealt with.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Unilateral + offensive + chronic maxillary sinusitis in an adult = odontogenic; polyps and atrophic rhinitis are bilateral, and pansinusitis is not one-sided.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.97. Foetid-discharge list and the laterality rule from `L31) nasal symptoms 1.pdf`; the 12-week definition and the dental trigger from `L27.2) Chronic Sinusitis.pdf`; ozaena and nasal patency from `L29) Rhinitis 1.pdf`. **The same vignette is printed by the endpoint bank at 12 weeks with the same key (`entep-nose-66`); menus differ, so the two are held separately.** Secondary chapter: `ent-facialpain` (the referred otalgia).' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-342', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-facialpain',
  stem:'Which of the following is the typical site of **sphenoid sinusitis pain**?',
  options:['Cheek','Forehead','Teeth','Occipital area'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**The occipital area — sinus pain is felt over the sinus, and the sphenoid is the deepest and most posterior sinus of all.**\n\n**The sphenoid sits in the body of the sphenoid bone, behind every other sinus.** `L23) Anatomy of the Nose.pdf` places its drainage furthest back of the four: *"Sphenoid sinus opens in the sphenoethmoidal recess"*, behind and above the superior turbinate, while the frontal, anterior ethmoid and maxillary all open into the middle meatus and the posterior ethmoids into the superior meatus. Pain from a sinus that deep surfaces at the **back of the head** — occipital or vertex, often retro-orbital — and there is **no facial tenderness to press on**, which is exactly why sphenoiditis is the sinusitis that gets missed.\n\n**⚠️ The lecture names this topic but prints no words under it.** `L27.1) ACUTE rhinosinusitis.pdf` carries a slide headed *"Anatomical Location of Pain & Tenderness"* and a later one captioned *"Acute Sphenoiditis"* — both are **figures with no prose beneath them**, so the deck teaches the map by picture only. **The other bank prints the answer outright and is preferred here to outside knowledge:** `entep-nose-72` asks this same question and keys **occipital region**.\n\n| Option | Which sinus it belongs to |\n|---|---|\n| **Cheek** | **maxillary** — the anterior wall of the sinus is the cheek, so pain and tenderness are felt there directly |\n| **Forehead** | **frontal** — `L32) nasal symptoms 2.pdf` gives the mechanism as *"vacuum headache" (morning pain over the forehead due to obstruction of the frontal recess during sleep)* |\n| **Teeth** | **maxillary** again, referred downwards: the floor of the sinus is the alveolus, and the upper first molar is the tooth most intimately related to it (`entep-nose-62`) |\n| **Occipital area** | ✅ **sphenoid** — the deepest sinus, its pain surfaces at the back of the head |\n\n**Bottom line: maxillary → cheek and upper teeth · frontal → forehead · ethmoid → between the eyes · sphenoid → occiput or vertex.** Carry `L32`’s warning with it: rhinogenic headache is controversial, most headache in ENT patients is a **primary** headache merely triggered by nasal irritation, and *"acute sinusitis causes facial pain rather than headache"*.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Sinus pain is referred over the sinus; the sphenoid is the deepest and most posterior, so its pain is occipital or vertex, with no facial tenderness.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.97. Sphenoethmoidal drainage from `L23) Anatomy of the Nose.pdf`; vacuum headache and the primacy of primary headache from `L32) nasal symptoms 2.pdf`. **⚠️ `L27.1) ACUTE rhinosinusitis.pdf` heads a slide "Anatomical Location of Pain & Tenderness" but prints only a figure under it — a heading, not coverage.** The sphenoid–occiput pairing is taken from the endpoint bank’s printed key (`entep-nose-72`) in preference to outside knowledge.' }
,

{ id:'enthd-nose-343', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-sinuscomp',
  stem:'Which of the following is the most common paranasal sinus that causes **intracranial complications in children**?',
  options:['Frontal','Maxillary','Ethmoid','Sphenoid'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The ethmoid — because in a child it is the sinus that is actually there.** The whole question turns on the two words *in children*, and the adult answer to the same question is different.\n\n**Why the ethmoid, at this age.** The ethmoid air cells are pneumatised **at birth**; the frontal sinus does not begin to pneumatise until roughly the seventh or eighth year and is not adult-sized until the late teens *(the pneumatisation timetable is not taken from the course material — no cached ENT deck prints it)*. A sinus that does not yet exist cannot be the commonest source of anything, which leaves the ethmoid as the child’s principal infected sinus and therefore the principal exporter of infection in every direction — upward through the thin **fovea ethmoidalis** and **cribriform plate** into the anterior cranial fossa, and laterally through the **lamina papyracea** into the orbit.\n\n**The other bank prints the paediatric half of this outright and is preferred here to outside knowledge.** `entep-nose-34` keys the **ethmoid** as the sinus that most likely causes complications when inflamed, and its second printed box states plainly that *"orbital complications of rhinosinusitis are more frequent **in children than in adults** because of the thin lamina papyracea"*. `entep-nose-104` keys **orbital cellulitis** as the commonest complication of acute ethmoid sinusitis in a child. `L27.1) ACUTE rhinosinusitis.pdf` illustrates the same scenario with a **10-year-old girl** whose viral URTI worsened into a complicated bacterial sinusitis.\n\n| Option | Why not, in a child |\n|---|---|\n| **Frontal** | the classic intracranial offender — subdural empyema, frontal lobe abscess, Pott’s puffy tumour — but in **adolescents and adults**, once the sinus exists. `entep-nose-11` keys **frontal lobe abscess** in a 60-year-old with recent rhinosinusitis |\n| **Maxillary** | the most often infected sinus, but it drains **away** from the cranium; its neighbours are the orbital floor, the cheek and the alveolus, so its complications are local and dental |\n| **Ethmoid** | ✅ present from birth, roofed by paper-thin bone, adjacent to both orbit and anterior fossa |\n| **Sphenoid** | anatomically the most dangerous of all — cavernous sinus, optic nerve, pituitary — but sphenoiditis is **uncommon**, and rarer still in children. Dangerous is not the same as common |\n\n**⚠️ Record, and keep the key: the sequence in a child usually stops at the orbit.** Both endpoint items key an **orbital** complication for the paediatric ethmoid, and the progression runs preseptal cellulitis → orbital cellulitis → subperiosteal abscess → orbital abscess → cavernous sinus thrombosis. The key is defensible because the ethmoid is still the child’s commonest **source**; asked of an adult, the answer would be **frontal**.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'In children the ethmoid is pneumatised from birth while the frontal is not, so the ethmoid is the commonest source of both orbital and intracranial spread; in adults the answer becomes frontal.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.97. **⚠️ Neither rhinosinusitis deck teaches complications in prose** — `L27.1) ACUTE rhinosinusitis.pdf` captions a figure *"Orbital Complication"* and nothing more, and *cavernous sinus thrombosis* appears in `L30) Rhinitis 2.pdf` only under acute invasive fungal sinusitis. Grounded instead on the endpoint bank’s printed keys and boxes (`entep-nose-34`, `entep-nose-104`, `entep-nose-11`) in preference to outside knowledge; the pneumatisation timetable is tagged in the explanation as outside the course material.' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-344', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-sinusitis',
  stem:'All of the following are **symptoms** of acute maxillary sinusitis **except**?',
  options:['Cheek pain','Cheek tenderness','Cheek edema','Dullness on trans-illumination'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**Dullness on trans-illumination — it is the only item on the list the patient cannot report, because it is a bedside TEST rather than anything felt or seen.**\n\n**⚠️ Read the key on the word *symptom*, not on the clinical content.** A maxillary sinus full of pus really does fail to transilluminate, so dullness is a **true finding** in acute maxillary sinusitis — it is not false, it is simply not a symptom. Cheek pain is what the patient complains of; cheek tenderness and cheek oedema are elicited or seen at the first glance. Dullness needs a darkened room and a light held against the palate or the infraorbital rim. **The key is defensible on that technicality and is kept as printed; the discrepancy is recorded here rather than disputed.**\n\n**What the deck does require before the diagnosis is made.** `L27.1) ACUTE rhinosinusitis.pdf` sets a counting rule: *"In a patient with **two or more major** signs or symptoms, **one major and two minor**, or greater than three minor signs or symptoms, the diagnosis of acute rhinosinusitis is strongly suspected."* Its diagnostic triad is *"History · Clinical findings · Investigations"*, and its endoscopic step looks for *"congestion & mucopurulent discharge"*, *"pus in the middle meatus"* and *"pus in the sphenoethmoidal recess"* — **endoscopy, not transillumination**, is the examination the lecture teaches.\n\n| Option | What kind of finding it is |\n|---|---|\n| **Cheek pain** | a **symptom** — the anterior wall of the maxillary sinus is the cheek, and `entqb-nose4-164` characterises sinus pain as **dull, aching, worse on stooping and localised over the sinus** |\n| **Cheek tenderness** | a **sign**, but one produced by pressing the cheek — the deck’s slide *"Anatomical Location of Pain & Tenderness"* pairs the two |\n| **Cheek edema** | a **sign**, and a warning one: visible swelling raises the question of spread beyond the sinus |\n| **Dullness on trans-illumination** | ✅ keyed — a **test result**, and the one item of the four that no patient can volunteer |\n\n**Bottom line: transillumination is an obsolete bedside test, replaced by endoscopy and CT.** `L27.1` states that CT *"is not essential to diagnose uncomplicated acute rhinosinusitis"* but *"is certainly an important diagnostic tool in assessing for potential complications"*; `entep-nose-23` keys **CT** as the radiological investigation of choice for chronic disease. **Trans-illumination is printed by no cached ENT deck at all** — it survives in question banks, not in the lecture course.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Cheek pain, tenderness and oedema are the clinical features of acute maxillary sinusitis; dullness on transillumination is a bedside test result, not a symptom — and the test itself has been replaced by endoscopy and CT.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.98. Diagnostic counting rule, endoscopic findings and the role of CT from `L27.1) ACUTE rhinosinusitis.pdf`. **⚠️ ARGUABLE KEY, KEPT AS PRINTED: dullness on transillumination is a genuine finding in acute maxillary sinusitis; it is excluded only because it is a test result rather than a symptom, and the stem uses "symptoms" loosely for the other three as well (two of them are signs). Recorded, not disputed.** *Trans-illumination* returns zero across all cached ENT lectures.' }
,

{ id:'enthd-nose-345', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-sinusitis',
  stem:'Which of the following is the best cut for **CT scan** in order to identify **chronic rhinosinusitis**?',
  options:['Axial cut','Coronal cut','Sagittal cut','Transverse cut'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**The coronal cut — it is the only plane that lays the ostiomeatal complex out in one slice, and chronic rhinosinusitis is a disease of that channel.**\n\n**What the decks establish.** `L27.2) Chronic Sinusitis.pdf` makes CT the imaging test and rules out the alternative: *"MRI for diagnosis is discouraged because of increased cost and (overdiagnosis) in comparison to **CT without contrast**"*, and *"an important role of CT imaging in CRS with or without polyps is to exclude aggressive infections or neoplastic disease that might mimic CRS or ARS"*. `L27.1) ACUTE rhinosinusitis.pdf` limits it in acute disease: *"it is not essential to diagnose uncomplicated acute rhinosinusitis"*. Both banks agree on the modality — `entep-nose-23` and `entqb-nose4-158` key **CT scan** for chronic rhinosinusitis.\n\n**⚠️ Which plane is a gap in the cached material and is filled here rather than declared** — *coronal* returns **zero** across every cached ENT lecture. *(The following is not taken from the course material.)* A coronal section is cut in the plane of the face, so it displays, on one image and side by side: the **uncinate process**, the **ethmoid infundibulum**, the **bulla ethmoidalis**, the **middle turbinate** and the **middle meatus** — that is, the whole **ostiomeatal complex**, whose obstruction is the mechanism of the disease. It also shows the two boundaries the surgeon must not cross: the **lamina papyracea** laterally and the **cribriform plate / fovea ethmoidalis** above. And it reproduces the view the endoscope gives at operation, which is why coronal CT is the standard pre-FESS study.\n\n| Option | What it shows, and why it is second best |\n|---|---|\n| **Axial cut** | good for the **anteroposterior** extent — sphenoid, posterior ethmoid, orbital apex, optic canal — but it slices *along* the drainage pathway instead of across it |\n| **Coronal cut** | ✅ the ostiomeatal complex, the skull base and the lamina papyracea in one plane |\n| **Sagittal cut** | best for the **frontal recess** and the frontonasal drainage pathway; a supplementary reconstruction, not the primary study |\n| **Transverse cut** | **⚠️ this is the axial plane under a second name** — *transverse* and *axial* are the same section. The menu therefore prints one plane twice, which cannot be a distinct answer. Recorded as printed |\n\n**Bottom line: chronic rhinosinusitis is an obstruction problem, so image the obstruction.** Modern scanners acquire a volume and reformat in all three planes, but the coronal remains the one the report and the surgeon read first.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Coronal CT is the standard study in chronic rhinosinusitis because it displays the whole ostiomeatal complex, the lamina papyracea and the skull base in one plane, matching the endoscopic view.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.98. CT-not-MRI and the role of imaging from `L27.2) Chronic Sinusitis.pdf`; the acute-disease caveat from `L27.1) ACUTE rhinosinusitis.pdf`; modality confirmed by `entep-nose-23` and `entqb-nose4-158`. **⚠️ *coronal* returns zero across all cached ENT lectures — the choice of plane is filled from general knowledge and tagged as such in the explanation. ⚠️ The printed menu offers "Axial cut" and "Transverse cut", which are the same plane under two names; recorded as printed, key unaffected.**' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-346', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-sinusitis',
  stem:'Acute rhinosinusitis is characterized by all of the following **except**?',
  options:['Nose is the most common source of infection','It’s usually preceded by acute viral rhinosinusitis','Its duration is < 4 months','Treatment is mainly conservative'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The duration. Acute rhinosinusitis is measured in WEEKS, not months — under 4 weeks, not under 4 months.**\n\n**The time ladder the course uses.** `L27.2) Chronic Sinusitis.pdf` fixes the far end: chronic rhinosinusitis is *"chronic inflammatory disease of sinus mucosa lasting **more than 12 weeks**"*. The other bank prints the middle rung: `entqb-nose4-149` keys **4 to 12 weeks** as subacute rhinosinusitis. Acute is therefore everything **under 4 weeks**, and *"< 4 months"* would swallow subacute and part of chronic whole.\n\n| Stage | Duration |\n|---|---|\n| **Acute** | **< 4 weeks** |\n| **Subacute** | 4–12 weeks (`entqb-nose4-149`) |\n| **Chronic** | > 12 weeks (`L27.2`) |\n| **Recurrent acute** | repeated episodes, each < 1 month, with ≥10 symptom-free days between (`entqb-nose3-98`) |\n\n**Why the other three are true, in the lecture’s own words.**\n\n- **The nose is the source.** `L27.1) ACUTE rhinosinusitis.pdf` opens on precisely this: *"The nasal mucosa is **contiguous** with that of the paranasal sinuses, any inflammation of the sinuses is almost always accompanied by inflammation of the nasal cavity"* — which is why the disease is called rhino**sinusitis** at all, and the same continuity argued in `enthd-nose-348`.\n- **It follows a viral URTI.** The deck’s pathophysiology slide begins with *"Upper Respiratory tract infection"*, and it quantifies both directions: *"up to **80 %** of these URTIs may be associated with rhinosinusitis"*, while of about a billion viral cases a year *"only **0.5 % to 2.0 %** are complicated by bacterial infection"*.\n- **Treatment is mainly conservative.** That 0.5–2 % figure is the argument: *"we must distinguish between **viral** infections, **which resolve without antibiotic treatment**, and bacterial infections, which may require antimicrobial therapy"*, and *"intranasal corticosteroids are recommended for the treatment of ARS, both in moderate (**monotherapy**) and severe (with oral antibiotics) disease"*. The deck spends four slides on antibiotic **misuse** and on resistance mechanisms, and closes with two cautionary cases of inadequate and inappropriate prescribing.\n\n**Bottom line: an acute rhinosinusitis that has lasted months is not acute — it has been reclassified by the calendar.** Getting the ladder right is what decides whether the patient needs reassurance, an antibiotic, or a CT and a surgeon.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Acute rhinosinusitis lasts under 4 weeks (subacute 4–12, chronic over 12); it follows a viral URTI, arises from the contiguous nasal mucosa, and is treated conservatively in the great majority of cases.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.98. Contiguity, URTI pathophysiology, the 0.5–2 % bacterial figure and intranasal-steroid monotherapy all transcribed from `L27.1) ACUTE rhinosinusitis.pdf`; the 12-week chronic definition from `L27.2) Chronic Sinusitis.pdf`. **The subacute 4–12-week rung is printed by Grade Gain (`entqb-nose4-149`) and taken from there in preference to outside knowledge; no cached deck prints the acute end of the ladder in words.**' }
,

{ id:'enthd-nose-348', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-sinusitis',
  stem:'An 18-year-old female patient presents to the rhinology clinic with a complaint of severe headache together with cough and colored nasal discharge. She reports that she had a common cold infection **10 days ago**. What’s your provisional diagnosis?',
  options:['Acute bacterial sinusitis','Acute bacterial rhinosinusitis','Chronic bacterial rhinosinusitis','Acute viral rhinosinusitis'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**Ten days of symptoms that have not resolved is the bank’s own threshold for bacterial infection — and the right name for it is rhinoSINUSITIS, not sinusitis.**\n\n**⚠️ The menu prints "Acute bacterial sinusitis" and "Acute bacterial rhinosinusitis" side by side, and choosing between them IS the question.** `L27.1) ACUTE rhinosinusitis.pdf` opens by defining the disease as *"an inflammatory condition involving **the nose and paranasal sinuses**"*, and gives the reason on the same slide: *"the nasal mucosa is **contiguous** with that of the paranasal sinuses; any inflammation of the sinuses is **almost always accompanied by inflammation of the nasal cavity**"*. `L29) Rhinitis 1.pdf` says it a second time in a slide whose whole point is the naming: *"As the lining of the nose and paranasal sinuses is **continuous**, inflammatory processes tend to involve both areas → **Rhinosinusitis**"*. Isolated *sinusitis* is a term the course has deliberately retired. This patient’s coloured **nasal** discharge is the nasal half of the disease, on display in the stem.\n\n**The 10-day rule, and its sibling.** `L27.1` lists four factors suggesting bacterial infection — *"worsening of the symptoms after five days · **persistence of symptoms for more than 10 days** · presence of symptoms out of proportion to those typically associated with viral URTI · worsening of symptoms after initial improvement (**double-sickening**)"*. **`enthd-nose-331` is this exact vignette at 4 days and keys acute VIRAL rhinosinusitis; it carries the full rule and the timeline table — go there rather than repeating it here.** The two questions differ in one number, and that number is the entire diagnosis.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **Acute bacterial sinusitis** | right timing, **wrong name** — it excludes the nose, and the nasal discharge in the stem is nasal inflammation |\n| **Acute bacterial rhinosinusitis** | ✅ >10 days without resolution, coloured discharge, after a cold |\n| **Chronic bacterial rhinosinusitis** | needs **>12 weeks** (`L27.2) Chronic Sinusitis.pdf`); ten days is not chronic |\n| **Acute viral rhinosinusitis** | the answer at **days 1–9** with improving symptoms — the diagnosis in `enthd-nose-331`. Most colds resolve in *"5-10 days"* (`L29`), and only *"0.5 % to 2.0 %"* of viral cases turn bacterial |\n\n**Bottom line: the calendar decides.** Under 10 days and improving = viral. Over 10 days, or worsening after five, or worse again after getting better = bacterial. Cross-references: `entqb-nose4-156` (12 days with double-sickening, keyed bacterial), `enthd-nose-346` (the duration ladder).\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Symptoms persisting beyond 10 days after a cold mark acute BACTERIAL rhinosinusitis; the term rhinosinusitis is preferred to sinusitis because the nasal and sinus mucosa are one continuous lining.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.98. The four bacterial indicators and the contiguity definition transcribed from `L27.1) ACUTE rhinosinusitis.pdf`; the continuity slide from `L29) Rhinitis 1.pdf`; the 12-week chronic threshold from `L27.2) Chronic Sinusitis.pdf`. **⚠️ Deliberate contrast pair with `enthd-nose-331` — the identical vignette at 4 days, keyed acute viral, which carries the full timeline; do not merge them. ⚠️ The menu prints both "Acute bacterial sinusitis" and "Acute bacterial rhinosinusitis"; the key is the latter on the contiguity argument.**' }
,

{ id:'enthd-nose-349', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-sinusitis',
  stem:'Which of the following organisms is the **most common cause of acute bacterial rhinosinusitis**?',
  options:['Streptococcus pneumoniae','Hemophilus influenza','Moraxella catarrhalis','Staphylococcus aureus'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**Streptococcus pneumoniae heads the list, and the first three options are the list — in order.**\n\n**The three community-acquired organisms, ranked:**\n\n| Organism | Rank and character |\n|---|---|\n| ***Streptococcus pneumoniae*** | ✅ **the commonest**; the same pneumococcus that heads acute otitis media (`entep-ear-47`), which is the same mucosa in a different cavity |\n| ***Haemophilus influenzae*** | **second**, and frequently **β-lactamase producing** |\n| ***Moraxella catarrhalis*** | **third**, relatively commoner in children, and almost always β-lactamase producing |\n| ***Staphylococcus aureus*** | ✅ not a cause of ordinary community-acquired acute rhinosinusitis. `L27.2) Chronic Sinusitis.pdf` places it in the **chronic** disease instead, listing *"Bacteria (especially **Staph. aureus endotoxins**)"* among the proposed triggers of CRS |\n\n**The other bank prints the same trio and is preferred here to outside knowledge:** `entqb-nose4-152` asks which organism does **not** cause acute rhinosinusitis and offers *Haemophilus influenza*, *Streptococcus influenza* (a printed defect — no such organism) and *Moraxella catarrhalis* against *Pseudomonas aeruginosa*. Both banks therefore print the same three-organism list from opposite directions.\n\n**Why the ranking is the clinically useful part.** `L27.1) ACUTE rhinosinusitis.pdf` never names an organism, but it spends its whole treatment section on the consequence: resistance develops through *"bacterial production of enzymes such as **beta-lactamase** that destroy the antibiotic"*, and the antibiotic should be chosen by *"**rates of resistance within the country**"*. Because *H. influenzae* and *M. catarrhalis* commonly make β-lactamase while the pneumococcus does not, plain amoxicillin covers the commonest organism but can fail against the second and third — which is the entire argument for **amoxicillin-clavulanate**. The deck’s cautionary case is a 10-year-old girl given a clavulanate dose *"only 84 mg while she is in need of 180 mg"*, who failed treatment and needed endoscopic drainage.\n\n**Bottom line: pneumococcus first, then the two β-lactamase producers — and the ranking is what your antibiotic has to cover.** *Staphylococcus aureus* belongs to chronic disease and biofilm; *Pseudomonas* to the nosocomial, cystic-fibrosis and immunocompromised sinus.\n\n**Note as printed:** option b is spelled *"Hemophilus influenza"*; the organism is ***Haemophilus influenzae***. Kept as the bank prints it.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Acute bacterial rhinosinusitis: S. pneumoniae first, H. influenzae second, M. catarrhalis third; S. aureus belongs to chronic rhinosinusitis, and β-lactamase in the second and third is why clavulanate is added.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.99. **⚠️ `L27.1) ACUTE rhinosinusitis.pdf` prints NO organism list** — it teaches resistance mechanisms and antibiotic choice instead, both quoted above; *Staph. aureus* in chronic disease is from `L27.2) Chronic Sinusitis.pdf`. The three-organism list is taken from Grade Gain’s printed menu (`entqb-nose4-152`) in preference to outside knowledge; the ordering itself is this bank’s own printed key. **Option b is printed "Hemophilus influenza" and kept as printed.**' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-350', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-noseanat',
  stem:'Which of the following is the **main function of nose**?',
  options:['Olfactory function','Respiratory function','Phonatory function','Mucociliary clearance function'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**Mucociliary clearance — the function whose failure causes disease, and the one the entire respiratory lining is built to perform.**\n\n**What the lecture prints, and the tension inside this menu.** `L23) Anatomy of the Nose.pdf` lists the functions in order:\n\n> *"**Respiratory function: Air way, purification of air.** Olfactory function: in the olfactory area. Phonatory function: resonance of voice. Other functions: Lacrimal fluid drainage. Reflex function."*\n\n**⚠️ Record the tension and keep the key.** *"Purification of air"* **is** mucociliary clearance, so on the deck’s own wording the keyed option is a **component of the respiratory function** that option b also names — the menu offers the whole and one of its parts, and keys the part. **The other bank resolves it the same way and is preferred here to outside knowledge:** `entqb-nose1-28` asks *"the main function of the nose is"* and keys **mucociliary clearance** over air warming, olfaction and voice resonance. Two banks, two different menus, the same key. Kept as printed.\n\n**The structural argument for the key.** The respiratory area — *"the rest of the nasal cavity"* — is *"lined by pseudo stratified columnar **ciliated** with **goblet cells**"* (`L23`). Goblet cells lay down the mucus blanket; the cilia sweep it backwards to the nasopharynx to be swallowed, carrying trapped particles, bacteria and irritants with it. `L29) Rhinitis 1.pdf` puts the same system at the head of the nose’s defences: *"Defence-Mechanisms: **1. Mucociliary system** ✓, 2. Epithelial integrity, 3. Reflexes (cough, sneeze), 4. Lysozymes, opsonins & complement, 5. Immunoglobulins (secretory IgA, IgE), 6. Macrophages & lymphocytes."* It is listed first, and it is the only one ticked.\n\n| Option | Its place |\n|---|---|\n| **Olfactory function** | real, but confined to *"the upper part of the nose related to the superior turbinate, cribriform plate"*, lined by non-ciliated columnar cells (`enthd-nose-238`); anosmia is disabling, not life-limiting |\n| **Respiratory function** | the **category** the key sits inside — airway plus purification of air; the trap of the question |\n| **Phonatory function** | *"resonance of voice"*; its failure gives the nasal voice of obstruction, not disease of the airway |\n| **Mucociliary clearance function** | ✅ keyed — the mechanism of purification, and the one that fails clinically |\n\n**Bottom line: clearance is what the nose is FOR, and its failure is what fills the sinus.** `L27.1) ACUTE rhinosinusitis.pdf` lists *"ciliary impairment"* among the predisposing factors to rhinosinusitis and names its causes — *"viral infection · smoking · allergy · **primary ciliary dyskinesia**"*. Stop the cilia and you get sinusitis; that is the argument for the key in one line.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'The nose’s main function is mucociliary clearance — the mucus blanket of the goblet cells swept back by the ciliated respiratory epithelium; ciliary impairment is a named predisposing factor for rhinosinusitis.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.99. Function list and respiratory epithelium from `L23) Anatomy of the Nose.pdf`; the defence-mechanism list from `L29) Rhinitis 1.pdf`; ciliary impairment as a predisposing factor from `L27.1) ACUTE rhinosinusitis.pdf`. **⚠️ ARGUABLE KEY, KEPT AS PRINTED: the deck’s own list names "Respiratory function: Air way, purification of air" FIRST, and purification of air IS mucociliary clearance — so this menu offers a category (option b) and one of its components (option d), and keys the component. Grade Gain’s `entqb-nose1-28` keys mucociliary clearance too, on a menu that does not offer "respiratory function". Recorded, not disputed.**' }
,

{ id:'enthd-nose-351', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-rhin',
  stem:'Which of the following is the most effective **medical** treatment of nasal allergy?',
  options:['Topical antihistamines','Oral antihistamines','Nasal decongestants','Topical steroids'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**Topical steroids — the only class in the lecture’s own drug matrix that scores against ALL FOUR symptoms of allergic rhinitis.**\n\n`L29) Rhinitis 1.pdf` prints a *Drug and Symptoms Matrix* and the answer can be read straight off it (stars as printed):\n\n| Drug class | Sneezing | Itching | Rhinorrhea | Congestion |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Antihistamines | ***** | **** | *** | — |\n| Anticholinergics (ipratropium bromide) | — | — | ***** | — |\n| **Corticosteroids** | ***** | ***** | *** | *** |\n| Decongestants | — | — | — | ***** |\n| Mast cell stabilisers | ***** | *** | * | — |\n| Antileukotrienes | *** | ** | — | **** |\n\n**Corticosteroids are the only row with no gap.** Everything else is a partial agent: antihistamines leave **congestion** untreated, decongestants treat congestion and nothing else. The other bank keys the same conclusion outright — `entqb-nose3-112`, *"which drug is commonly used to treat **all symptoms** of allergic rhinitis"*, keys **intranasal corticosteroids**.\n\n**Why topical rather than systemic.** Grade Gain’s printed box at `entqb-nose3-113` states it in one line: *"we usually use **local** corticosteroids to avoid side effects"*, and the same box explains the pharmacological superiority — *"antihistamines affect the first cascade of mast cells (early response), while corticosteroids affect the late response **and** the early response, as it has anti-histaminic and anti-inflammatory effects"* (see `enthd-nose-357`).\n\n| Option | Why it is not the most effective |\n|---|---|\n| **Topical antihistamines** | fast onset in the nose, but the antihistamine row still has an empty **congestion** column — and congestion is the symptom patients present with |\n| **Oral antihistamines** | same ceiling, plus systemic effects; best as an **adjunct** to a topical steroid, which is the combination keyed in `enthd-nose-357` |\n| **Nasal decongestants** | powerful for congestion alone, and self-limiting: `L31) nasal symptoms 1.pdf` restricts topical drops to *"not more than 5 days to avoid development of **rhinitis medicamentosa**"*, and `L30) Rhinitis 2.pdf` defines that as rebound congestion from *"prolonged excessive use"*. A drug you must stop within a week is not a treatment for a chronic allergy |\n| **Topical steroids** | ✅ the full symptom spectrum, given locally |\n\n**⚠️ Contrast this with `enthd-nose-362`, which asks the *best line of management* and keys ALLERGEN AVOIDANCE.** The two are not in conflict: `L29`’s management slide is ordered *"Allergen Avoidance · Pharmacotherapy · Immunotherapy"*, so avoidance comes first overall, and the topical steroid is the best **drug** once avoidance has been arranged. **Read whether the stem says *medical* or *management*.**\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Intranasal corticosteroids are the most effective drug in allergic rhinitis because they are the only class covering sneezing, itching, rhinorrhoea and congestion; antihistamines miss congestion and decongestants must be stopped within five days.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.99. Drug/symptom matrix and the three-step management order from `L29) Rhinitis 1.pdf`; the five-day decongestant limit from `L31) nasal symptoms 1.pdf`; rhinitis medicamentosa from `L30) Rhinitis 2.pdf`. Grade Gain corroborates at `entqb-nose3-112` and prints the local-versus-systemic rationale in the box at `entqb-nose3-113`. **⚠️ Deliberate contrast with `enthd-nose-362` (best line of management = allergen avoidance) — the discriminator is the word *medical*.**' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-352', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-rhin',
  stem:'A 35-year-old female patient has been suffering from **seasonal nasal obstruction** for the last few years. This obstruction is usually accompanied by **sneezing and watery nasal discharge**. What’s the most likely diagnosis?',
  options:['Atrophic rhinitis','Hypertrophic rhinitis','Allergic rhinitis','Rhinosinusitis'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**Allergic rhinitis — the stem recites the lecture’s definition almost word for word, and the word *seasonal* names the allergen.**\n\n`L29) Rhinitis 1.pdf` defines it as *"an **Ig.E mediated hypersensitivity** of nasal mucous membrane characterized by: **sneezing, itching, watery rhinorrhea and a sensation of nasal obstruction**"*, occurring *"in atopic individuals who are exposed to common aero-allergens"*. Three of those four features are printed in the stem. The same deck lists **seasonal rhinitis** first among its allergen categories and illustrates it with a photograph captioned **grass pollen** — pollen is the only allergen with a calendar, which is why a complaint that returns every year at the same time is allergic until proved otherwise (`entqb-nose3-129`, `entep-nose-10`).\n\n| Option | Why it fails |\n|---|---|\n| **Atrophic rhinitis** | the discharge is not watery but *"scanty **viscid** secretions that rapidly dry to form **crusts** that emit a characteristic **foul odor (ozaena)**"*, and the nose is abnormally **patent**, not obstructed. It is progressive and year-round, never seasonal |\n| **Hypertrophic rhinitis** | *"an advanced stage of non-specific chronic [rhinitis] with **permanent** whole mucosa hypertrophy"*, with *"thick, nodular mucosa especially at the posterior end of the inferior turbinate (**mulberry like**)"* — permanent means **constant**, not seasonal, and there is no sneezing or watery discharge |\n| **Allergic rhinitis** | ✅ seasonal, sneezing, watery rhinorrhoea, obstruction |\n| **Rhinosinusitis** | the discharge would be **mucopurulent** and the dominant complaint facial pain or pressure; `L31) nasal symptoms 1.pdf` files mucopurulent discharge under rhinosinusitis and watery bilateral discharge under **allergic rhinitis** or the early common cold |\n\n**The external signs to look for on examination** (`L29`): *"allergic salute · mouth breathing · **allergic shiners** · pale bluish edematous nasal turbinates"*, and intranasally *"polyps, septal deflection or prominent nasal turbinates"*. The pale, boggy turbinate is the finding that separates the allergic nose from the red, angry mucosa of infection.\n\n**Bottom line: seasonal + sneezing + watery = allergic, every time.** By the ARIA clock this patient may be **intermittent** or **persistent** depending on how long her season runs (`enthd-nose-366`) — the trigger names the allergen, the time course names the class. Compare `enthd-nose-359`, the same diagnosis reached from a spring-and-summer history with an allergic salute.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Seasonal nasal obstruction with sneezing and watery rhinorrhoea is allergic rhinitis — an IgE-mediated hypersensitivity; atrophic and hypertrophic rhinitis are year-round and produce crusts or permanent turbinate hypertrophy instead.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.99. Definition, allergen categories, external signs, and the atrophic and hypertrophic descriptions all transcribed from `L29) Rhinitis 1.pdf`; discharge types from `L31) nasal symptoms 1.pdf`. **⚠️ NEAR-TWIN ACROSS BANKS, NOT FOLDED: the endpoint bank prints the same vignette in a 30-year-old female (`entep-nose-5`) with a different menu (rhinoscleroma and CSF rhinorrhoea in place of hypertrophic rhinitis and rhinosinusitis) and the same key; both are held.**' }
,

{ id:'enthd-nose-354', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-septum',
  stem:'A child fell on his face while playing football. He started to develop **nasal obstruction 2 days after the fall** which made him seek medical advice. Examination revealed **soft red swelling located on nasal septum**. What’s the most likely diagnosis?',
  options:['Nasal septal hematoma','Nasal septal abscess','Angiofibroma','Intranasal meningocele'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**A septal swelling two days after trauma, with no fever and no systemic upset, is blood — not yet pus.**\n\n**What it is.** `L26) nasal masses.pdf` defines it exactly: *"Septal haematoma refers to the **collection of blood under the mucoperichondrium or mucoperiosteum** of nasal septal cartilage or bone. It may be unilateral or bilateral, with the latter being **more frequent in the setting of severe trauma**"*, and files it among the **midline** nasal masses. `L24) diseases of the nasal septum.pdf` makes looking for it a routine part of assessing a nasal injury: *"look for **septal hematoma, abscess**"*.\n\n**The discriminator is TIME plus SEPSIS, and the other bank prints both halves.** `entqb-nose7-210` — trauma to the nose, *"there is **no fever**"* — keys **septal hematoma**. `entep-nose-8` — a 5-year-old *"**four days** later, nasal obstruction **and fever**"* — keys **septal abscess**. This boy is at **two days with no fever offered**, which puts him on the haematoma side of that pair.\n\n| Option | Why it fails, or fits |\n|---|---|\n| **Nasal septal hematoma** | ✅ post-traumatic, days-old, midline septal swelling, obstructing both sides |\n| **Nasal septal abscess** | the same swelling **infected** — later, with **fever**, severe pain and systemic upset. The stem prints neither. **⚠️ *red* is the one word that leans the other way; it is not enough against the timing and the absent fever, and the diagnoses are consecutive stages of one process rather than alternatives** |\n| **Angiofibroma** | wrong site, wrong sex-age pattern, wrong trigger: `L26` places it *"in the confluence of posterolateral nasal wall and lateral nasopharynx (centered on SPF)"*, in *"males during adolescence"*, presenting with **epistaxis** and unilateral obstruction, never after a fall |\n| **Intranasal meningocele** | **congenital** and present from birth, not a new swelling after trauma. `L26` classifies it as a **roof** lesion, where *"intracranial tissue herniates through a defect in the cranium"*; it enlarges on crying or straining and transilluminates |\n\n**Bottom line: treat it as an emergency, not an observation.** `entep-nose-25` keys **immediate surgical evacuation**, and `entep-nose-101` records that the one untrue statement about septal haematoma is that it is *"treated by decongestants and anti-histamines"*. The reason is the blood supply: the septal cartilage is avascular and feeds through the perichondrium, so a haematoma that lifts the perichondrium off starves the cartilage. Untreated it goes two ways — **abscess** within days, or **cartilage necrosis and saddle nose** (`entqb-nose6-191`, which keys neglected nasal haematoma alongside Wegener’s granulomatosis as causes of saddle deformity).\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'A midline septal swelling within days of nasal trauma and without fever is a septal haematoma; it needs immediate evacuation, because untreated it becomes an abscess or necroses the cartilage into a saddle nose.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.100. Haematoma definition and the meningocele and angiofibroma descriptions transcribed from `L26) nasal masses.pdf`; the examination instruction from `L24) diseases of the nasal septum.pdf`. **The haematoma-versus-abscess discriminator (time and fever) is taken from the other banks’ printed keys — `entqb-nose7-210` (no fever → haematoma) and `entep-nose-8` (4 days + fever → abscess) — in preference to outside knowledge; no cached deck prints septal abscess in prose.** ⚠️ The word *red* in the stem argues mildly for early infection; recorded, key kept.' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-355', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-nasalobs',
  stem:'A 22-year-old male patient presents to the ENT clinic with **unilateral watery nasal discharge that increases on coughing or bending over**. Which of the following can be the cause of this discharge?',
  options:['Allergic rhinitis','Common cold','CSF leak','All of the above'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**CSF leak — and the point of the question is that "All of the above" is wrong, because the other two are BILATERAL.**\n\n`L31) nasal symptoms 1.pdf` splits watery discharge by laterality and the split is the whole answer:\n\n> ***Nasal Discharge: Watery*** *— "**Unilateral:** 1. **CSF rhinorrhea**. **Bilateral:** 1. Allergic rhinitis. 2. Early stages of acute viral rhinitis (common cold)."*\n\n**Allergic rhinitis and the common cold are watery, but they are not one-sided.** Both act on mucosa that lines both nasal cavities, so both noses run. A watery discharge confined to one side has to come from a **hole**, not from inflammation. `L32) nasal symptoms 2.pdf` reinforces it from the other direction with a true/false slide whose answer is false: *"CSF Rhinorrhea is typically bilateral."*\n\n**Why coughing and bending make it worse.** Those manoeuvres raise **intracranial pressure**, which drives more fluid through a skull-base defect. That is a positional and provocable leak — a feature no mucosal cause can imitate, and the reason the history alone is nearly diagnostic.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **Allergic rhinitis** | watery, but **bilateral**, and accompanied by sneezing and itching (`L29) Rhinitis 1.pdf`) |\n| **Common cold** | watery only in the **early** stage, then mucopurulent; **bilateral**, with fever, sore throat and malaise |\n| **CSF leak** | ✅ unilateral, clear, increased by straining, stooping or coughing |\n| **All of the above** | ✅ the trap — it is right about *watery* and wrong about *unilateral* |\n\n**`L26) nasal masses.pdf` puts this symptom on its alarm list**: among *"alarming symptoms"* it prints *"persistent unilateral **watery rhinorhea**"* alongside persistent unilateral obstruction, epistaxis and sinus pain. Unilateral and persistent is the pattern that must not be treated as rhinitis.\n\n**Bottom line: unilateral clear watery discharge is CSF until proved otherwise.** Both other banks key it identically — `entep-nose-59` and `entep-nose-28` on the bare symptom, `entep-nose-73` and `entqb-nose5-184` on vignettes. The reason it matters is `entqb-nose5-179`: the most feared complication of CSF rhinorrhoea is **meningitis**, because the leak is an open door into the subarachnoid space.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Watery nasal discharge that is unilateral and worsens on coughing or stooping is a CSF leak; allergic rhinitis and the common cold are watery but bilateral.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.100. The unilateral/bilateral watery-discharge split transcribed from `L31) nasal symptoms 1.pdf`; the true/false correction from `L32) nasal symptoms 2.pdf`; the alarm list from `L26) nasal masses.pdf`. The intracranial-pressure mechanism behind the positional increase is printed by no cached deck; both other banks key the same diagnosis (`entep-nose-59`, `entep-nose-28`, `entep-nose-73`, `entqb-nose5-184`).' }
,

{ id:'enthd-nose-356', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-nasalmass',
  stem:'Which of the following is the ideal management of **allergic nasal polyps** in order to **minimize recurrence**?',
  options:['Nasal endoscopic polypectomy','Nasal endoscopic polypectomy and ethmoidectomy with steroids','Open technique surgical polypectomy','Transmaxillary ethmoidectomy'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**Because polyps grow from the ethmoid, removing the visible polyp alone leaves the factory standing — you must open the ethmoid and then treat the mucosa that made them.**\n\n**Where polyps come from.** `L26) nasal masses.pdf` prints the origins as percentages, and every site on the list is ethmoidal or at the ethmoid’s doorway: *"80 % uncinate – MT – infundibulum · 66 % ant face of bulla – hiatus · 48 % frontal recess · 42 % between bulla & MT · 30 % inside bulla · 15 % middle turbinate · 10 % post ethmoids – sup turbinate"*. Grade Gain keys the same conclusion: `entqb-nose7-217` gives the **ethmoidal air cells** as the commonest site.\n\n**Why steroids are part of the answer and not an afterthought.** Polyposis is an inflammatory diathesis, not a lump. `L26` lists medical treatment as *"topical nasal steroids · systemic oral steroids · antibiotics · antihistamines · steroid sparing medications"*, and puts surgery only after *"failed adequate medical R"*. `L27.2) Chronic Sinusitis.pdf` states the goal of surgery in words that make the combination one manoeuvre: *"remove obstruction, reduce inflammation, and **improve delivery of topical meds**"* — the surgery exists partly to let the steroid reach the mucosa. It also allows *"a short course of oral corticosteroids… (only in **CRSwNP**)"*.\n\n| Option | Why it recurs, or does not |\n|---|---|\n| **Nasal endoscopic polypectomy** | removes what is visible in the nasal cavity and leaves the **ethmoid** mucosa that generated it, with no anti-inflammatory afterwards — the highest recurrence of the four |\n| **Polypectomy + ethmoidectomy with steroids** | ✅ clears the generator, ventilates the sinuses, and continues topical steroid on the mucosa; this is what *functional* endoscopic sinus surgery means |\n| **Open technique surgical polypectomy** | an external approach for a bilateral mucosal disease — more morbidity, an external scar, and no better clearance. `entep-nose-17` and `entqb-nose4-159` both key **FESS**, not an open procedure, for disease failing medical treatment |\n| **Transmaxillary ethmoidectomy** | reaches the ethmoid through the maxillary sinus, an obsolete route superseded by the endoscope; it also skips the steroid arm entirely |\n\n**Bottom line: recurrence is the natural history of nasal polyposis, and the answer is designed to lower it, not abolish it.** `L27.2` warns that the aspirin-triad patient in particular has *"significantly worse radiological CT findings… difficult to treat and… a **higher rate of recurrence**"* (`enthd-nose-364`). That is what the patient should be told before consenting: surgery buys airway and smell, steroids keep them.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Nasal polyps arise from ethmoid mucosa, so minimising recurrence needs endoscopic polypectomy plus ethmoidectomy plus continued steroids — polypectomy alone leaves the generating mucosa behind.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.100. Origin percentages, medical treatment list and surgical indications transcribed from `L26) nasal masses.pdf`; the goals of surgery and the oral-steroid rule from `L27.2) Chronic Sinusitis.pdf`. Corroborated by `entqb-nose7-217` (ethmoid as commonest site) and by `entep-nose-17` / `entqb-nose4-159` (FESS after failed medical therapy). **No cached deck names transmaxillary ethmoidectomy; it is described from general knowledge and identified as such above.**' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-357', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-rhin',
  stem:'Which of the following is true regarding the role of **antihistamines and corticosteroids** in management of allergic rhinitis?',
  options:['Antihistamines stop the early phase of allergy','Corticosteroids stop both the early and delayed allergic phases','Topical corticosteroids together with oral antihistamines is the best line of management of allergic rhinitis','All of the above'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**All three statements are true, and together they are the argument for the standard prescription: an intranasal steroid with an oral antihistamine.**\n\n**The first two statements are printed almost verbatim by the other bank**, whose box at `entqb-nose3-113` is preferred here to outside knowledge:\n\n> *"Antihistamines affect the first cascade of mast cells (**early response**). While corticosteroids affect the **late response and the early response**, as it has anti-histaminic and anti-inflammatory effects. We usually use **local** corticosteroids to avoid side effects."*\n\n**The mechanism behind the split.** `L29) Rhinitis 1.pdf` prints the allergic cascade as a chain — *"Sensitization → IgE production → **arming of mast cells** → release of mediators → clinical effects"*. An antihistamine intercepts **one mediator at one moment**: the histamine released when the armed mast cell degranulates, which produces the early symptoms of sneezing and itch (`entqb-nose3-91`). A corticosteroid acts upstream on transcription, suppressing the cytokines and the **eosinophil** recruitment that build the late phase — so it covers both ends of the reaction.\n\n| Statement | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **Antihistamines stop the early phase** | ✅ true — histamine is the early-phase mediator; sneezing and itch respond fastest |\n| **Corticosteroids stop both phases** | ✅ true — anti-histaminic **and** anti-inflammatory, which is why they are the only class in `L29`’s drug matrix scoring against all four symptoms (`enthd-nose-351`) |\n| **Topical corticosteroid + oral antihistamine is the best line** | ✅ true — the steroid covers congestion and the late phase, the antihistamine gives fast relief of sneezing and itch, and giving the steroid **topically** avoids systemic effects |\n| **All of the above** | ✅ keyed |\n\n**The practical corollary is a timing point worth telling the patient.** An oral antihistamine works within an hour, so it suits occasional and predictable exposure; an intranasal steroid takes **days** to reach full effect and must be used regularly, so it suits persistent or moderate-to-severe disease (`enthd-nose-366`). A patient who expects the spray to work like a tablet abandons it on day two.\n\n**⚠️ This is *pharmacotherapy*, which is the SECOND step.** `L29`’s management slide orders the whole approach *"Allergen Avoidance · Pharmacotherapy · Immunotherapy"* — see `enthd-nose-362`.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Antihistamines block the early, histamine-driven phase; corticosteroids block both the early and late phases; a topical steroid with an oral antihistamine is therefore the standard combination.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.100. **⚠️ The early/late-phase split is printed by NO cached ENT deck** — it is taken from Grade Gain’s own printed explanation box at `entqb-nose3-113` in preference to outside knowledge. The allergic cascade, the drug/symptom matrix and the three-step management order are from `L29) Rhinitis 1.pdf`. Onset-of-action timing is general pharmacology, identified as such above.' }
,

{ id:'enthd-nose-358', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-nasalmass',
  stem:'A 6-year-old child with **unilateral nasal mass and obstruction** together with discharge comes to your clinic. What is the **next step in management**?',
  options:['Excision biopsy','Antibiotic therapy','CT scan','Watchful waiting'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**Image before you touch it. A unilateral nasal mass in a child may be brain, and the CT is what tells you before a biopsy causes a CSF leak or meningitis.**\n\n**Unilateral is the alarming word.** `L26) nasal masses.pdf` closes on a list headed *"Alarming symptoms"*: *"persistent **unilateral** progressive nasal obstruction · persistent unilateral watery rhinorhea · persistent unilateral epistaxis · persistent unilateral sinus pain · extranasal manifestations"*. `L31) nasal symptoms 1.pdf` says the same from the classification side — *"**unilateral** nasal obstruction usually points to structural abnormality or uni-lateral pathology"* — and lists the paediatric causes: *"choanal atresia / pyriform aperture stenosis · **congenital masses and cysts** · foreign bodies · adenoids · rhinitis and allergy · nasal polyps (cystic fibrosis)"*.\n\n**Why imaging is the *next* step and not a later one.** `L26` makes investigation the point of the preliminary diagnosis — *"importance of preliminary diagnosis: urgency of dealing with the problem · **determines needed investigations** · **applicability of office procedures**"* — and prints the radiological classification the scan supplies: *"**CT**: bone like or soft tissue like · **MRI**: intensity signal compared to muscle"*. Among its midline and roof lesions is the **encephalocele**, where *"intracranial tissue herniates through a defect in the cranium"*. That is the lesion the CT is there to exclude, because it is the one that must never be biopsied or avulsed in the clinic.\n\n| Option | Why it fails |\n|---|---|\n| **Excision biopsy** | the classic disaster: a *"polyp"* in a small child that is actually an encephalocele or a vascular lesion. `entqb-nose7-205` keys **investigations for a meningocele** ahead of polypectomy in a 3-year-old with a unilateral polyp, for exactly this reason |\n| **Antibiotic therapy** | treats the discharge and ignores the mass. It would delay a diagnosis without changing it |\n| **CT scan** | ✅ defines bone versus soft tissue, shows any skull-base defect, and plans the approach |\n| **Watchful waiting** | forbidden by the alarm list — *persistent unilateral* is the pattern that must not be observed. `entep-nose-39` likewise keys **CT scan** for a unilateral nasal mass at the other end of life |\n\n**Bottom line: a unilateral nasal mass is a radiological diagnosis before it is a histological one.** In a child of this age the realistic differential is a **foreign body** (`entep-nose-71`, `entep-nose-100`), an **antrochoanal polyp** (`entqb-nose7-227`) or a congenital midline lesion — and the scan separates the three without risk.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'A unilateral nasal mass in a child is imaged before it is biopsied, because CT distinguishes a foreign body or antrochoanal polyp from a congenital encephalocele with a skull-base defect.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.101. Alarm list, the role of preliminary diagnosis, the CT/MRI classification and the encephalocele description transcribed from `L26) nasal masses.pdf`; paediatric causes and the laterality rule from `L31) nasal symptoms 1.pdf`. Corroborated by `entqb-nose7-205` (investigate for meningocele before polypectomy in a child) and `entep-nose-39` (CT for a unilateral nasal mass).' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-359', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-rhin',
  stem:'A 20-year-old female patient complains of bilateral watery nasal discharge, sneezing especially in the morning and **repeated nasal rub**. She reports that this condition becomes **more pronounced during spring and summer**. What is the most probable diagnosis?',
  options:['Seasonal allergic rhinitis','Perennial allergic rhinitis','1ry atrophic rhinitis','2ry atrophic rhinitis'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**Spring and summer is a pollen calendar, and the repeated nasal rub is the allergic salute — both point to SEASONAL allergic rhinitis.**\n\n**The two clinching details are both printed by the deck.** `L29) Rhinitis 1.pdf` lists its allergen categories with *"**Seasonal rhinitis**"* first and illustrates the slide with a photograph captioned **grass pollen** — the only allergen with a season. Its external-signs slide opens with *"**allergic salute** · mouth breathing · **allergic shiners** · pale bluish edematous nasal turbinates"*: the salute *is* the repeated upward rub of the nose with the palm, and the stem describes it without naming it. The definition supplies the rest — *"sneezing, itching, **watery rhinorrhea** and a sensation of nasal obstruction"* in an atopic individual exposed to aero-allergens.\n\n| Option | Why it fails, or fits |\n|---|---|\n| **Seasonal allergic rhinitis** | ✅ symptoms confined to spring and summer = a pollen season |\n| **Perennial allergic rhinitis** | the identical symptoms **all year round**, because the allergen is indoors and continuous — house dust mite above all (`enthd-nose-360`). The stem’s seasonality excludes it |\n| **1ry atrophic rhinitis** | the opposite picture: *"progressive atrophy of nasal mucosa and the underlying bone of the turbinates"* with *"scanty **viscid** secretions that rapidly dry to form **crusts**"* and *"abnormal **patency** of nasal passages"* — foul odour, epistaxis and crusting, no sneezing, no watery discharge, no season. `L29` gives its primary causes as chronic bacterial infection (Klebsiella, pertussis, diphtheria), nutritional deficiency, oestrogen deficiency and autoimmunity |\n| **2ry atrophic rhinitis** | the same end picture arising from an identifiable insult — *"excessive surgical destruction of nasal mucosa · chronic exposure to irritant, cocaine or toxic agents · healed stage of granulomatous disease, T.B., scleroma · radiotherapy"*. Nothing in the history offers one |\n\n**⚠️ Do not translate *seasonal* into *intermittent* automatically.** The ARIA classes count time, not pollen: intermittent is *"< 4 days/week **or** < 4 weeks consecutive/y"*, persistent *"> 4 days/week **and** > 4 weeks consecutive/y"* (`enthd-nose-366`). A patient symptomatic every day across two full seasons is **seasonal** by trigger and **persistent** by clock. The two labels answer different questions and this stem asks for the trigger.\n\n**Bottom line: the salute plus a calendar is enough.** Compare `enthd-nose-352`, the same diagnosis reached from a seasonal history without the salute.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Symptoms confined to spring and summer, with the allergic salute, are seasonal (pollen-driven) allergic rhinitis; perennial disease is year-round, and atrophic rhinitis gives crusts and a roomy nose instead.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.101. Allergen categories, the grass-pollen illustration, the external-signs list, the definition, and both the primary and secondary atrophic-rhinitis aetiologies all transcribed from `L29) Rhinitis 1.pdf`. The ARIA classes are on the same deck’s classification slide. **The identification of "repeated nasal rub" WITH the allergic salute is inference from the deck’s own term, not a separate printed statement.**' }
,

{ id:'enthd-nose-360', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-rhin',
  stem:'Which of the following is the most common allergen of **perennial** nasal allergy?',
  options:['House dust mites','Grass pollen','Tree pollen','Floor dust'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**House dust mites — the only allergen on this list that is in the air every day of the year, because it lives in the bed.**\n\n**What the deck gives, and what it does not.** `L29) Rhinitis 1.pdf` divides allergens into *"seasonal rhinitis · **perennial allergic rhinitis** · occupational allergens · food and drug induced rhinitis · role of pollution"* and illustrates the slide with a photograph captioned **grass pollen**. **⚠️ It names the perennial category but never names its allergen** — *mite* returns **zero** across the whole deck, and across every other nose lecture. **Both other banks print the missing name, and are preferred here to outside knowledge:** the endpoint box at `entep-nose-10` states that *"**house dust mites** cause **perennial** symptoms"*, in contrast with pollens; Grade Gain’s `entqb-nose3-129` keys pollen for intermittent disease and files dust mite, animal dander and mould as the year-round, persistent triggers.\n\n| Option | Exposure | Pattern |\n|---|---|---|\n| **House dust mites** | in bedding, mattresses, carpets and soft furnishing **all year** | ✅ **perennial** |\n| **Grass pollen** | a defined flowering season | seasonal — the deck’s own captioned example |\n| **Tree pollen** | an even shorter, earlier season | seasonal |\n| **Floor dust** | ⚠️ a lay term, and the trap: **house dust is not itself the allergen**. The sensitising proteins are in mite faecal pellets, and to a lesser degree animal dander and mould spores carried in the dust *(the composition of house dust is not taken from the course material)* |\n\n**Why the distinction changes treatment, not just the label.** `L29` heads its management with **allergen avoidance**, and avoidance means something entirely different for the two groups. Against pollen it is a matter of timing — an intranasal steroid started before the season and stopped after it. Against mite it is domestic and continuous: mite-proof mattress and pillow covers, hot washing of bedding, removing carpets and soft toys from the bedroom, reducing humidity. **Perennial exposure never lets up, so perennial treatment cannot be seasonal.**\n\n**Bottom line: pollen has a calendar, mite does not.** That is the whole of the seasonal/perennial split — and it is a different axis from the ARIA intermittent/persistent clock, which counts the patient’s days rather than the allergen’s season (`enthd-nose-366`, `enthd-nose-359`).\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'House dust mite is the commonest perennial aeroallergen; pollens are seasonal. The distinction dictates whether avoidance is a calendar measure or a permanent domestic one.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.101. The seasonal/perennial allergen categories, the grass-pollen illustration and the avoidance-first management order are from `L29) Rhinitis 1.pdf`. **⚠️ *mite* returns ZERO across every cached ENT lecture — the deck names the perennial category without naming its allergen. Filled from the other banks’ printed material (`entep-nose-10`’s explanation box and `entqb-nose3-129`) in preference to outside knowledge; the composition of house dust is tagged in the explanation as outside the course material.**' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-361', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-rhin',
  stem:'All of the following **immune cells are involved in the pathogenesis of allergic rhinitis** **except**?',
  options:['Plasma cells','Mast cells','Eosinophils','Basophils','Macrophages'],
  answer:4,
  explanation:'**Macrophages — they are a general nasal defence in this course, not a step in the allergic cascade the lecture prints.**\n\n**The cascade, in the deck’s own words.** `L29) Rhinitis 1.pdf` prints *The Allergic reaction* as a five-step chain: *"**Sensitization** → **Ig E Production** → **Arming of mast cells** → **Release of mediators** → **Clinical effects**"*, on top of a definition that calls the disease *"an **Ig.E mediated** hypersensitivity of nasal mucous membrane"*. Every keyed-in cell maps onto a step of that chain:\n\n| Cell | Its step in the chain |\n|---|---|\n| **Plasma cells** | the terminally differentiated B cells that **produce the IgE** — step two of the printed cascade |\n| **Mast cells** | *"arming of mast cells"* is printed verbatim; cross-linking of their surface IgE degranulates them and releases **histamine**, the early-phase mediator |\n| **Eosinophils** | the cell of the **late phase**, and the deck relies on them diagnostically: its investigations slide uses **blood and nasal-smear eosinophils**, warning they are *"neither sensitive or specific"* and are also raised in *"non-allergic eosinophilic rhinitis"* (NARES) and *"parasitic infection"* |\n| **Basophils** | the circulating counterpart of the mast cell, IgE-armed and histamine-releasing |\n| **Macrophages** | ✅ **keyed as the exception** — `L29` lists them only under **defence mechanisms**: *"1. Mucociliary system · 2. Epithelial integrity · 3. Reflexes (cough, sneeze) · 4. Lysozymes, opsonins & complement · 5. Immunoglobulins (secretory IgA, IgE) · **6. Macrophages & lymphocytes**"* — general innate defence of the nose, printed nowhere in the allergic cascade |\n\n**⚠️ Record the tension and keep the key.** In immunology proper, **sensitization requires an antigen-presenting cell**, and macrophages and dendritic cells are the ones that take up the allergen and present it to T-helper cells to drive IgE class-switching *(this is not taken from the course material)*. So macrophages are not biologically irrelevant to allergy. **The key is defensible on what the course prints:** the deck assigns macrophages to defence and the other four cells to the reaction, and it is the deck’s division that the question is testing.\n\n**Bottom line: plasma cell makes the IgE, mast cell and basophil release the histamine, eosinophil builds the late phase.** That is the four-cell allergic quartet — and it maps exactly onto treatment, since antihistamines cover the mast-cell moment and corticosteroids cover the eosinophilic one (`enthd-nose-357`).\n\n**Note the format:** this stem prints **five** options, not four.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Plasma cells, mast cells, basophils and eosinophils are the cells of the IgE cascade in allergic rhinitis; the course assigns macrophages to the nose’s general defence mechanisms instead.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.101. **A five-option stem.** The allergic cascade, the IgE definition, the eosinophil investigations slide and the six defence mechanisms (macrophages among them) are all transcribed from `L29) Rhinitis 1.pdf`. **⚠️ Key defensible on the deck’s own division, and kept: antigen presentation by macrophages and dendritic cells genuinely initiates sensitization, which is tagged in the explanation as outside the course material.**' }
,

{ id:'enthd-nose-362', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-rhin',
  stem:'What is the **best line of management** of allergic rhinitis?',
  options:['Oral antihistamines','Topical steroids','Systemic steroids','Minimizing exposure to the allergen'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**Allergen avoidance — the lecture puts it first, and it is the only measure that removes the cause instead of blunting the response.**\n\n`L29) Rhinitis 1.pdf` heads its treatment section *Management of Allergic Rhinitis* and prints exactly three steps, in this order:\n\n> *"**Allergen Avoidance** · **Pharmacotherapy** · **Immunotherapy**"*\n\n**No drug on this menu addresses the allergen; avoidance is the only option that does.** Allergic rhinitis is *"an Ig.E mediated hypersensitivity… in **atopic individuals who are exposed to common aero-allergens**"* — remove the exposure and the cascade of `enthd-nose-361` never starts. That is why it is step one and why every pharmacological answer is downstream of it.\n\n| Option | Where it sits |\n|---|---|\n| **Oral antihistamines** | pharmacotherapy, and only the **early phase** — the drug matrix leaves its **congestion** column empty (`enthd-nose-351`) |\n| **Topical steroids** | the **most effective drug**, covering all four symptoms and both phases — but still step two, and still a suppressant |\n| **Systemic steroids** | reserved and short-course: `L27.2) Chronic Sinusitis.pdf` allows oral steroids only after topical failure and *"only in CRSwNP"*. Long-term systemic steroids for a chronic allergy are unacceptable |\n| **Minimizing exposure to the allergen** | ✅ step one of the deck’s own list |\n\n**⚠️ Read this against `enthd-nose-351`, which keys TOPICAL STEROIDS on the same page of the same bank.** The two are not in conflict, and spotting why is the exam skill: that question asks for the most effective **medical** treatment, so it is confined to step two; this one asks for the best line of **management**, which opens the whole list. **One word of the stem moves the answer.** Grade Gain sets the same trap from the other side — `entqb-nose3-130` offers antihistamines, intranasal corticosteroids and avoidance of allergens and keys **all of the above**.\n\n**What avoidance means in practice depends on the allergen.** For pollen it is a calendar measure — start the intranasal steroid before the season (`enthd-nose-359`). For house dust mite it is domestic and permanent: mite-proof bedding covers, hot washing, removing carpets from the bedroom (`enthd-nose-360`). **Advice a patient cannot carry out is not avoidance**, which is why pharmacotherapy follows in almost every real case rather than replacing it.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'The management of allergic rhinitis runs allergen avoidance, then pharmacotherapy, then immunotherapy — avoidance is the only step that removes the cause.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.102. The three-step management order, the definition and the drug/symptom matrix are transcribed from `L29) Rhinitis 1.pdf`; the restriction on oral steroids from `L27.2) Chronic Sinusitis.pdf`. **⚠️ Deliberate contrast with `enthd-nose-351` (most effective MEDICAL treatment = topical steroids), printed three pages earlier in the same bank — the discriminator is *medical* versus *management*.** Corroborated by `entqb-nose3-130`.' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-364', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-rhin',
  stem:'**Samter’s triad** includes all of the following **except**?',
  options:['Aspirin allergy','Bronchial asthma','Allergic rhinitis','Nasal polyps'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**Allergic rhinitis. A triad has three members — aspirin intolerance, bronchial asthma and nasal polyps — and allergic rhinitis is the background these patients sit in, not one of the three.**\n\n**⚠️ The lecture’s own sentence is loosely worded and does mention allergic rhinitis; record it and keep the key.** `L29) Rhinitis 1.pdf` prints, on its intranasal-signs slide: *"**Allergic rhinitis** with diffuse sinonasal polyposis + asprin hypersensitivity and brnchial asthma → (**Samtter’s Triad**)"* (the deck’s own spellings — read *aspirin*, *bronchial*, *Samter’s*). Taken literally that names four things. **The other bank settles it and is preferred here:** `entqb-nose3-132` asks for the characteristic feature of Samter’s triad and keys *"**Bronchial asthma, sinonasal polyps, and aspirin intolerance**"* — three members, allergic rhinitis not among them.\n\n**The course’s second name for it makes the same point.** `L27.2) Chronic Sinusitis.pdf` calls it *"**Aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease (AERD, Samter’s triad)**"* and files it under **other types** of chronic rhinosinusitis: *"often very extensive, with significantly worse radiological CT findings than non-AERD patients with nasal polyps. They can be **difficult to treat** and have a **higher rate of recurrence**."* The name contains **aspirin**, **respiratory** and **disease** — the three limbs, with no allergy in it.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **Aspirin allergy** | member — strictly an **intolerance**, not an IgE allergy at all; skin-prick and specific IgE testing to aspirin are useless |\n| **Bronchial asthma** | member — and `L29` notes separately that *"allergic rhinitis and bronchial asthma frequently coexist"*, which is a different observation |\n| **Allergic rhinitis** | ✅ keyed as the exception — the setting the polyps grow in, not a limb of the triad |\n| **Nasal polyps** | member — *"diffuse sinonasal polyposis"* in the deck’s words, and `L29` files nasal polyposis under **AR & co-morbidities** |\n\n**⚠️ Do not confuse it with the ATOPIC triad**, which is asthma + allergic rhinitis + **eczema** (`entqb-nose3-94`). **Asthma is the only member the two share**, and *that* triad is the one allergic rhinitis belongs to — which is exactly why it is the tempting distractor here.\n\n**Bottom line: the recognition changes the consent conversation.** It marks the polyp patient whose disease will be extensive on CT, resistant to treatment and prone to **recur after surgery** (`enthd-nose-356`), and for whom **all non-selective NSAIDs** are contraindicated, not aspirin alone *(the NSAID class effect is not taken from the course material)*.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Samter’s triad (AERD) is aspirin intolerance + asthma + nasal polyps; allergic rhinitis belongs to the atopic triad with eczema, not to this one.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.102. The triad sentence is transcribed with the deck’s spellings from `L29) Rhinitis 1.pdf`; the AERD name and prognosis from `L27.2) Chronic Sinusitis.pdf`. **⚠️ THE DECK AND THE KEY DISAGREE ON WORDING: `L29` writes "Allergic rhinitis with diffuse sinonasal polyposis + aspirin hypersensitivity and bronchial asthma → Samter’s Triad", which reads as four members. Recorded, key kept — Grade Gain’s `entqb-nose3-132` prints the three-member form and is followed here.** Contrast the atopic triad at `entqb-nose3-94`. The NSAID class effect is tagged in the explanation as outside the course material.' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-365', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-rhin',
  stem:'Allergic rhinitis is characterized by which of the following types of **antibodies and hypersensitivity reactions**?',
  options:['IgE, type II hypersensitivity reaction','IgG, type I hypersensitivity reaction','IgE, type I hypersensitivity reaction','IgG, type II hypersensitivity reaction'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**IgE and type I — the immediate, mast-cell hypersensitivity, and the lecture names the antibody in its opening definition.**\n\n`L29) Rhinitis 1.pdf` defines allergic rhinitis as *"an **Ig.E mediated** hypersensitivity of nasal mucous membrane characterized by: sneezing, itching, watery rhinorrhea and a sensation of nasal obstruction"*, occurring *"in atopic individuals who are exposed to common aero-allergens"*. The type number follows from the mechanism the same deck prints as a chain: *"Sensitization → **Ig E Production** → **Arming of mast cells** → Release of mediators → Clinical effects"*. **IgE bound to mast cells, cross-linked by allergen, degranulating within seconds — that is the definition of type I.**\n\n| Option | Where it goes wrong |\n|---|---|\n| **IgE, type II** | right antibody, wrong type. **Type II** is antibody-dependent cytotoxicity, mediated by **IgG or IgM** against a cell-surface antigen — autoimmune haemolytic anaemia, Goodpasture, transfusion reactions. IgE has no part in it |\n| **IgG, type I** | right type, wrong antibody. Type I is IgE by definition; there is no IgG-mediated type I |\n| **IgE, type I** | ✅ both halves correct |\n| **IgG, type II** | a coherent pairing, but the wrong disease entirely — it names cytotoxic hypersensitivity, not allergy |\n\n**How the antibody is used clinically.** `L29`’s investigations slides run on it: skin allergy testing places allergen extract into the skin *"by either scratching or pricking… or by intradermal injection"*, and blood testing measures *"**total IgE**"* and *"**specific IgE (RAST test)**"*. It also prints the caution that keeps the test in proportion: *"about **50 %** of patients with allergic rhinitis have **normal** levels of total IgE, and **20 %** of non-affected individuals can have **elevated** total IgE levels"* — so a normal total IgE does not exclude the diagnosis and a raised one does not make it. **Specific IgE is the informative test; total IgE is not.**\n\n**Bottom line: type I = IgE = immediate = mast cell.** The other bank keys the same fact on a menu that tests only the antibody (`entqb-nose3-85`, *"IgE-mediated hypersensitivity of the nasal mucous membrane"*). **⚠️ Allergic FUNGAL sinusitis is the exception worth carrying**: `L30) Rhinitis 2.pdf` records that there *"**IgE type I immediate hypersensitivity and type III hypersensitivity** are involved"* — two mechanisms, not one.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Allergic rhinitis is an IgE-mediated type I (immediate, mast-cell) hypersensitivity; total IgE is normal in half of sufferers, so specific IgE or skin prick testing is what counts.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.102. Definition, allergic cascade, skin and blood testing, and the 50 %/20 % caveat all transcribed from `L29) Rhinitis 1.pdf`; the dual mechanism of allergic fungal sinusitis from `L30) Rhinitis 2.pdf`. **The Gell and Coombs numbering of types II and III is standard immunology and is printed by no cached ENT deck; identified as such in the explanation.** Corroborated by `entqb-nose3-85`.' },

{ id:'enthd-nose-366', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-rhin',
  stem:'Which of the following best defines **intermittent allergic rhinitis**?',
  options:['Symptoms persist for <4 days/week and <4 consecutive weeks/year','Symptoms persist for <4 days/week or <4 separate weeks/year','Symptoms persist for <4 days/week and <4 separate weeks/year','Symptoms persist for <4 days/week or <4 consecutive weeks/year'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**Fewer than 4 days a week OR fewer than 4 CONSECUTIVE weeks a year. This menu tests two words at once — the conjunction and the word *consecutive* — and only one option gets both right.**\n\n`L29) Rhinitis 1.pdf` prints the classification as a grid, and its wording is decisive on both points:\n\n| Class | The deck’s own words |\n|---|---|\n| **Intermittent** | *"**< 4 days/week Or < 4 weeks Consecutive/y**"* |\n| **Persistent** | *"**> 4 days /week & > 4 weeks Consecutive /y**"* |\n\n**Why *or* and not *and*.** To be called **persistent** the disease must be both frequent **and** sustained — the deck prints an ampersand there. Failing **either** condition is therefore enough to make it intermittent, which is why intermittent takes **or**. A patient with daily symptoms for a fortnight in the pollen season is frequent but not sustained → intermittent. A patient with two bad days a week all year is sustained but not frequent → intermittent too.\n\n**Why *consecutive* and not *separate*.** The count is of an unbroken run, not a tally of scattered weeks. Four separate bad weeks spread across a year is a very different illness from four in a row, and the deck writes *Consecutive* in both rows of its grid.\n\n| Option | Fault |\n|---|---|\n| **<4 days/week **and** <4 consecutive weeks** | right on *consecutive*, wrong conjunction — this demands both conditions and would misclassify the seasonal patient as persistent |\n| **<4 days/week **or** <4 **separate** weeks** | right conjunction, wrong on *separate* |\n| **<4 days/week **and** <4 **separate** weeks** | wrong on both |\n| **<4 days/week **or** <4 **consecutive** weeks** | ✅ both correct |\n\n**Severity is graded on a separate axis**, so every patient is classified twice — for example *intermittent, moderate-to-severe*. `L29` prints **mild** as *"normal sleep · no impairment of daily activities · no troublesome symptoms in untreated patients"* and **moderate to severe** as *"abnormal sleep · impairment of daily activity · abnormal work"*.\n\n**⚠️ Do not equate intermittent with seasonal.** The ARIA labels count the patient’s **time course**; seasonal and perennial name the **allergen** (`enthd-nose-360`, `enthd-nose-359`). A three-month pollen season produces persistent disease from a seasonal allergen.\n\n**Bottom line: the classification decides the drug.** Intermittent, mild disease is managed with avoidance and an as-needed antihistamine; persistent or moderate-to-severe disease needs a regular intranasal steroid (`enthd-nose-351`).\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'ARIA intermittent allergic rhinitis = symptoms on fewer than 4 days a week OR for fewer than 4 consecutive weeks a year; persistent requires both thresholds to be exceeded. Severity is graded separately.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.102. The classification grid and both severity bands transcribed verbatim from `L29) Rhinitis 1.pdf`. **⚠️ NEAR-DUPLICATE ACROSS BANKS, NOT FOLDED: Grade Gain’s `entqb-nose3-88` asks the same definition, but its menu varies only the conjunction and the inequality and never prints *consecutive* versus *separate*; this bank’s menu tests both words, so the two are held separately. Its mirror `entqb-nose3-89` asks the persistent half.**' }
